 {
   "id": "grammar.b1.guerrafria.04.so-pretexto-de",
-  "title": "So pretexto de: una justificación oficial cuestionable",
+  "title": "Reporting what governments and documents say",
   "sections": [
     {
       "type": "text",
-      "content": "*So pretexto de* (under the pretext of) introduces an official justification the writer is signaling as doubtful -- distinct from a neutral connector, it carries built-in skepticism about whether the stated reason was the real one. Exactly the tool for describing Cold War interventions whose declared goals rarely matched their actual motives. These sentences are taken directly from the previous reading."
+      "title": "What you already know",
+      "content": "You know how to report statements with *según* + a source and with *decir que* from B1 Unit 13, and how the tense moves back after a past verb (*dijo que quería*). This is useful for foreign interventions, where governments, documents and historians tell different stories."
+    },
+    {
+      "type": "table",
+      "title": "Three ways to report",
+      "rows": [
+        [
+          "*Según* + source + fact",
+          "*Según los documentos, la CIA organizó la operación.*"
+        ],
+        [
+          "*dijo que* + imperfect",
+          "*Dijo que quería proteger a los ciudadanos.*"
+        ],
+        [
+          "*El informe dice que* + present or past",
+          "*El informe dice que se dio dinero a los opositores.*"
+        ]
+      ]
     },
     {
       "type": "examples",
+      "title": "In context",
       "items": [
         {
-          "spanish": "La CIA organizó un golpe de Estado contra Árbenz, so pretexto de que su reforma agraria representaba una amenaza comunista para la región.",
-          "english": "The CIA organized a coup against Árbenz, under the pretext that his land reform represented a communist threat to the region."
+          "spanish": "Según documentos publicados después por el gobierno de Estados Unidos, la CIA organizó la operación.",
+          "english": "According to documents published later by the US government, the CIA organised the operation."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "So pretexto de restaurar el orden constitucional, Estados Unidos envió más de veinte mil soldados a la República Dominicana.",
-          "english": "Under the pretext of restoring constitutional order, the United States sent more than twenty thousand soldiers to the Dominican Republic."
+          "spanish": "El presidente Johnson dijo que quería proteger a los ciudadanos estadounidenses.",
+          "english": "President Johnson said that he wanted to protect US citizens."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "So pretexto de apoyar la autodeterminación de los pueblos, la URSS y Cuba proporcionaron entrenamiento militar a movimientos guerrilleros.",
-          "english": "Under the pretext of supporting peoples' self-determination, the USSR and Cuba provided military training to guerrilla movements."
+          "spanish": "El gobierno de Estados Unidos dijo que los comunistas tenían influencia sobre Árbenz.",
+          "english": "The US government said that communists had influence over Árbenz."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "Este patrón de intervención, justificada so pretexto de principios elevados, estaba motivado con frecuencia por cálculos mucho más pragmáticos.",
-          "english": "This pattern of intervention, justified under the pretext of lofty principles, was frequently motivated by far more pragmatic calculations."
+          "spanish": "Según el informe del Senado, hubo operaciones secretas contra Allende.",
+          "english": "According to the Senate report, there were secret operations against Allende."
         }
       ]
     },
     {
       "type": "tip",
-      "content": "*So pretexto de* is somewhat archaic and formal -- you'll mostly meet it in historical or legal writing, where it works exactly like bajo el pretexto de but in a more compact, literary register."
+      "content": "Use *según* + the source to keep a claim apart from your own voice: the source is responsible for it. After a past reporting verb (*dijo que*), the verb inside moves back: *quiero* becomes *quería*, *tienen* becomes *tenían*."
     }
   ]
 }
diff --git a/content/es-latam/grammar/b1/b1-guerrafria-05-no-hay-que-no-gr.json b/content/es-latam/grammar/b1/b1-guerrafria-05-no-hay-que-no-gr.json
