 {
   "id": "grammar.b1.guerrafria.05.no-hay-que-no",
-  "title": "No hay + sustantivo + que no: una afirmación universal",
+  "title": "Imperfect and present perfect: before and now",
   "sections": [
     {
       "type": "text",
-      "content": "No hay + noun + que no + subjunctive (\"there is no X that doesn't...\") is a double negative that reads as a sweeping universal claim -- the strongest way to say \"every single one\" without literally saying it, perfect for a closing lesson arguing that Cold War polarization touched absolutely everything. These sentences are taken directly from the previous reading."
+      "title": "What you already know",
+      "content": "You know the imperfect from A2 Unit 21 and the present perfect from A2 Unit 1. From B1 Unit 5 you also know how they contrast: the imperfect for how things used to be, the present perfect for a change that still matters now. A Cold War summary needs both: what the region was like then, and what historians have found since."
+    },
+    {
+      "type": "table",
+      "title": "Then and now",
+      "rows": [
+        [
+          "*Antes*, *ya*, *entonces* + imperfect",
+          "*Antes los documentos eran secretos.*"
+        ],
+        [
+          "*Hoy*, *en los últimos años* + present perfect",
+          "*Se han abierto archivos.*"
+        ]
+      ]
     },
     {
       "type": "examples",
+      "title": "In context",
       "items": [
         {
-          "spanish": "No hay institución política que no se viera obligada, tarde o temprano, a definirse frente a esta confrontación global.",
-          "english": "There is no political institution that wasn't eventually forced to take a stance on this global confrontation."
+          "spanish": "Antes de 1945, las universidades y los sindicatos ya tenían sus propios conflictos.",
+          "english": "Before 1945, universities and unions already had their own conflicts."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "No hay universidad latinoamericana de este periodo que no experimentara las mismas tensiones ideológicas que dividían al resto de la sociedad.",
-          "english": "There is no Latin American university from this period that didn't experience the same ideological tensions dividing the rest of society."
+          "spanish": "Los historiadores han podido estudiar documentos que antes eran secretos.",
+          "english": "Historians have been able to study documents that used to be secret."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "No hay prácticamente ningún sindicato obrero de la región que escapara a esta misma dinámica.",
-          "english": "There is practically no labor union in the region that escaped this same dynamic."
+          "spanish": "En los últimos años se han abierto archivos en Estados Unidos.",
+          "english": "In recent years archives have been opened in the United States."
         },
         {
-          "spanish": "No hay ejército latinoamericano de este periodo que no acabara adoptando algún elemento de la Doctrina de Seguridad Nacional.",
-          "english": "There is no Latin American military from this period that didn't end up adopting some element of the National Security Doctrine."
+          "spanish": "Antes se veía la política como una lucha entre dos bandos; hoy los historiadores han descubierto más causas.",
+          "english": "Before, politics was seen as a fight between two sides; today historians have discovered more causes."
         }
       ]
     },
     {
       "type": "tip",
-      "content": "The verb inside the que clause is always subjunctive here, since the sentence is describing a category (\"any X\") rather than one specific, known thing -- que no se viera, que no experimentara, que no acabara, all in the subjunctive."
+      "content": "Use the imperfect for the background (*eran secretos*, *tenían*) and the present perfect for what has changed up to now (*han podido*, *se han abierto*). One sentence can have both."
     }
   ]
 }
